1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,859
This video is about program understanding. We learn to draw object diagrams. An object diagram shows the state of a running Java program at one line.

2
00:00:11,309 --> 00:00:25,815
First we list what it means to understand a program. Then we learn the notation and five rules that execute one line by hand. At the end we run two story programs from the course notes, line by line.

3
00:00:26,265 --> 00:00:31,001
The course notes list six things that we know when we understand a program.

4
00:00:31,451 --> 00:00:33,393
One: the inputs of the program.

5
00:00:33,843 --> 00:00:37,213
Two: the outputs. The outputs include the errors.

6
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:42,549
Three: the static structure. Static means what you can see in the source text.

7
00:00:42,999 --> 00:00:48,695
Four: the dynamic execution. Dynamic means what happens when the program runs.

8
00:00:49,145 --> 00:00:50,809
Five: the algorithms.

9
00:00:51,259 --> 00:00:53,947
Six: the programming paradigm, or style.

10
00:00:54,397 --> 00:01:07,261
This matters in a compiler course for two reasons. First, a compiler is a program that understands other programs. It analyzes the programs that it compiles in some of these ways.

11
00:01:07,711 --> 00:01:14,111
Second, a compiler is a large program itself. You must understand the programs that you write.

12
00:01:14,561 --> 00:01:23,563
Static structure is data structures, class diagrams and design patterns. The slides say that you know this from earlier courses.

13
00:01:24,013 --> 00:01:29,987
Dynamic execution is the second group. The slides say that you must learn more about it.

14
00:01:30,437 --> 00:01:32,911
Control structures and the call graph.

15
00:01:33,361 --> 00:01:34,876
Pointers and aliasing.

16
00:01:35,326 --> 00:01:39,059
Object diagrams. They show the program execution.

17
00:01:39,509 --> 00:01:41,941
Mutation: which data changes.

18
00:01:42,391 --> 00:01:46,018
Invariants: data relationships that do not change.

19
00:01:46,468 --> 00:01:50,799
Algorithms is the third group. You must learn more about it too.

20
00:01:51,249 --> 00:01:54,513
The runtime, in big O and in constant factors.

21
00:01:54,963 --> 00:01:58,675
The space usage, in big O and in constant factors.

22
00:01:59,125 --> 00:02:03,861
And the termination conditions: the conditions that make the program stop.

23
00:02:04,311 --> 00:02:10,348
This video and the next one teach pointers, aliasing, object diagrams and mutation.

24
00:02:10,798 --> 00:02:19,694
First, a short refresher of object oriented terms, from the course notes. Here is a small Java program with two classes.

25
00:02:20,144 --> 00:02:22,704
B is a base class, or super class.

26
00:02:23,154 --> 00:02:27,314
D is a derived class, or sub class. D extends B.

27
00:02:27,764 --> 00:02:34,228
x is a field, or instance variable. Every object of class B has its own x.

28
00:02:34,678 --> 00:02:39,371
y is a local variable. It exists only during one call of foo.

29
00:02:39,821 --> 00:02:44,579
foo is a method. In C plus plus terms, it is a virtual method.

30
00:02:45,029 --> 00:02:48,762
The foo in class D overrides the foo in class B.

31
00:02:49,212 --> 00:03:00,049
Here is the order in which the course uses these topics. Before you start, you need procedural programming, data structures such as lists and trees, and classes.

32
00:03:00,499 --> 00:03:11,571
For the midterm: object diagrams with pointers and aliasing, inheritance and polymorphism, which means virtual methods, and the labs up to lab 4.

33
00:03:12,021 --> 00:03:18,720
For the final: design patterns, termination by iteration to a fixed point, and all the labs.

34
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:22,349
So object diagrams are midterm material.

35
00:03:22,799 --> 00:03:34,895
Example programs often use names with no meaning, such as foo and bar. The course notes use stories instead: super hero origin stories. They follow a pattern.

36
00:03:35,345 --> 00:03:38,843
First, the hero is young, and has no super powers.

37
00:03:39,293 --> 00:03:43,432
Second, tragedy comes to the hero's family, often from a villain.

38
00:03:43,882 --> 00:03:47,765
Third, the hero gets super powers, and perhaps an alias.

39
00:03:48,215 --> 00:03:53,079
Each part is a change of program state. Harry is a new Person object.

40
00:03:53,529 --> 00:03:56,281
The attack sets his father and mother to null.

41
00:03:56,731 --> 00:03:59,867
Superman is a second name for the same object.

42
00:04:00,317 --> 00:04:09,405
These programs have no input, no output and no algorithm. Their only purpose is to show program state, and how it changes.

43
00:04:09,855 --> 00:04:16,553
Here is the notation. The program counter, or P C, is the line that is about to execute.

44
00:04:17,003 --> 00:04:22,614
A stack frame has one slot for each local variable, and the parameters are included.

45
00:04:23,064 --> 00:04:27,907
There is one special frame for the static variables. Its name is statics.

46
00:04:28,357 --> 00:04:38,149
An object lives in the heap, the white space. Its name is the class name and a number that you make up. Only the keyword new makes an object.

47
00:04:38,599 --> 00:04:45,745
A field of one object can refer to another object. That is a solid arrow. The label is optional.

48
00:04:46,195 --> 00:04:53,555
A variable on the stack can refer to an object. That arrow is dotted. A solid line is also accepted.

49
00:04:54,005 --> 00:05:01,664
Two rules. First: the numbers have no meaning. Person 12 and Person 1005 are equally good.

50
00:05:02,114 --> 00:05:10,285
Second: every arrow ends in the heap. An arrow can start in the stack or in the heap, but it never ends at a stack slot.

51
00:05:10,735 --> 00:05:20,100
The course notes add one point. U M L object diagrams show only the heap. Our object diagrams also show the call stack.

52
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:31,217
The slides give five rules to execute one line by hand. Use them without thinking. They give the right answer even for code that you do not understand.

53
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:38,301
This is our own small example. Line 4 runs: new makes Person 1, and slot a points to it.

54
00:05:38,751 --> 00:05:45,642
Now line 5: b equals a. Rule 1 is for the right hand side. Here the right hand side is a.

55
00:05:46,092 --> 00:05:51,575
a is a name. Find its slot, and follow the arrow out of it. You get Person 1.

56
00:05:52,025 --> 00:05:58,126
Rule 2 is for the left hand side. Here it is b. b is a name, so find its slot.

57
00:05:58,576 --> 00:06:09,051
Rule 3: assign. First erase the old arrow out of slot b. Slot b holds null, and null means no arrow. So there is nothing to erase.

58
00:06:09,501 --> 00:06:13,085
Then draw the new arrow, from slot b to Person 1.

59
00:06:13,535 --> 00:06:17,204
Now a and b refer to the same object, Person 1.

60
00:06:17,654 --> 00:06:31,179
A common error is to draw an arrow from slot b to slot a. That is wrong. Arrows never go from stack to stack. The assignment copies the arrow, so b points to the object that a points to.

61
00:06:31,629 --> 00:06:37,197
Line 6: b dot spouse equals new Person. The right hand side is new.

62
00:06:37,647 --> 00:06:41,125
Rule 4: new is the only thing that makes a bubble.

63
00:06:41,575 --> 00:06:47,441
So a line with no new makes no bubble. Line 5 had no new, and it made no bubble.

64
00:06:47,891 --> 00:06:51,497
Here new makes Person 2. No arrow points to it yet.

65
00:06:51,947 --> 00:06:59,029
The left hand side is b dot spouse. Rule 2: resolve b. Follow its arrow to Person 1.

66
00:06:59,479 --> 00:07:02,103
Then take the field spouse of Person 1.

67
00:07:02,553 --> 00:07:11,663
Rule 3: draw the arrow from the spouse field of Person 1 to Person 2. It starts in the heap, so it is a solid arrow.

68
00:07:12,113 --> 00:07:17,275
Line 7 calls f with b. Rule 5: a call makes a new frame.

69
00:07:17,725 --> 00:07:26,429
The frame has one slot for each parameter and each local variable. f has one parameter, p, and no local variables.

70
00:07:26,879 --> 00:07:32,981
The argument is b. b points to Person 1, so p also points to Person 1.

71
00:07:33,431 --> 00:07:41,068
An alias is a second name for the same object. p is an alias of a and b: three names, one object.

72
00:07:41,518 --> 00:07:48,409
Line 10: p dot spouse equals null. Resolve p to Person 1, and take its field spouse.

73
00:07:48,859 --> 00:07:54,000
Erase the old arrow. The right hand side is null, so draw no new arrow.

74
00:07:54,450 --> 00:08:01,341
f returns. Rule 5: a return erases the frame of f, and every arrow that leaves it.

75
00:08:01,791 --> 00:08:13,695
The objects stay. No arrow goes into Person 2 now, so no slot can reach it. We draw it gray. It is still in the heap. The garbage collector decides when it goes.

76
00:08:14,145 --> 00:08:19,563
f changed the object of a and b, but the code of f never names a or b.

77
00:08:20,013 --> 00:08:24,963
Now the first story from the course notes: Harry Potter's origin story.

78
00:08:25,413 --> 00:08:28,335
Harry and Voldemort are static variables.

79
00:08:28,785 --> 00:08:30,407
main calls book 1.

80
00:08:30,857 --> 00:08:34,611
book 1 makes Harry, his father James and his mother Lily.

81
00:08:35,061 --> 00:08:39,243
Lines 12 to 15 connect them: father, mother and spouse.

82
00:08:39,693 --> 00:08:43,746
Then the program makes Voldemort, and Voldemort attacks Harry.

83
00:08:44,196 --> 00:08:50,447
Draw the object diagram at line 16 and at line 19. Pause the video and try it.

84
00:08:50,897 --> 00:08:59,985
Java does not show you two things. The first: static variables exist before main runs. So draw the statics frame first.

85
00:09:00,435 --> 00:09:05,235
Its slots, Harry and Voldemort, start as null. Null means no arrow.

86
00:09:05,685 --> 00:09:10,335
Then the runtime calls main. main has one local variable, args.

87
00:09:10,785 --> 00:09:15,415
args is never null. It points to an array of the command line words.

88
00:09:15,865 --> 00:09:17,657
The P C is line 6.

89
00:09:18,107 --> 00:09:25,872
Line 6 calls book 1. Rule 5: a new frame, with a slot for each local variable, James and Lily.

90
00:09:26,322 --> 00:09:29,757
book 1 is static, so it has no hidden this slot.

91
00:09:30,207 --> 00:09:32,447
The P C is now line 9.

92
00:09:32,897 --> 00:09:37,761
Line 9: Harry equals new Person. new makes a bubble, Person 1.

93
00:09:38,211 --> 00:09:43,736
Harry is not a local variable of book 1. So its slot is in the statics frame.

94
00:09:44,186 --> 00:09:47,087
Draw the arrow from slot Harry to Person 1.

95
00:09:47,537 --> 00:09:52,636
Line 10: new makes Person 2. Draw the slot James to Person 2.

96
00:09:53,086 --> 00:09:58,355
Line 11: new makes Person 3. Draw the slot Lily to Person 3.

97
00:09:58,805 --> 00:10:04,011
Every field of a new Person is null. So no arrows leave the three bubbles yet.

98
00:10:04,461 --> 00:10:14,423
Line 12: Harry dot father equals James. Rule 1, the right hand side: James. Follow the arrow of slot James to Person 2.

99
00:10:14,873 --> 00:10:21,743
Rule 2, the left hand side: Harry dot father. Harry leads to Person 1. Take its field father.

100
00:10:22,193 --> 00:10:29,745
Rule 3: draw the father arrow from Person 1 to Person 2. The line has no new, so it makes no bubble.

101
00:10:30,195 --> 00:10:36,573
Line 13: Harry dot mother equals Lily. Draw mother from Person 1 to Person 3.

102
00:10:37,023 --> 00:10:43,914
Line 14: James dot spouse equals Lily. Draw spouse from Person 2 to Person 3.

103
00:10:44,364 --> 00:10:51,447
Line 15: Lily dot spouse equals James. Draw spouse from Person 3 back to Person 2.

104
00:10:51,897 --> 00:10:57,529
This is object diagram 1, at P C 16. It matches the course notes.

105
00:10:57,979 --> 00:11:01,285
Count what it has. There are four bubbles in the heap.

106
00:11:01,735 --> 00:11:05,917
There are four slot arrows: Harry, args, James and Lily.

107
00:11:06,367 --> 00:11:10,569
There are four field arrows: father, mother, and two spouse arrows.

108
00:11:11,019 --> 00:11:14,262
Voldemort is still null, so it has no arrow.

109
00:11:14,712 --> 00:11:24,952
Line 17: Voldemort equals new Person. new makes Person 4. Voldemort is a static slot, so draw Voldemort to Person 4.

110
00:11:25,402 --> 00:11:31,951
Line 18 calls attack on Voldemort. Here is the second thing that Java does not show you.

111
00:11:32,401 --> 00:11:38,567
Every instance method has a hidden first parameter, called this. attack is an instance method.

112
00:11:39,017 --> 00:11:44,606
So the call means: attack, with this equal to Voldemort, and p equal to Harry.

113
00:11:45,056 --> 00:11:48,725
Rule 5: the attack frame has two slots, this and p.

114
00:11:49,175 --> 00:11:53,527
this gets the arrow of Voldemort. So this points to Person 4.

115
00:11:53,977 --> 00:11:57,924
p gets the arrow of Harry. So p points to Person 1.

116
00:11:58,374 --> 00:12:02,534
The P C is now line 27, the first line of attack.

117
00:12:02,984 --> 00:12:10,685
this is a slot like any other. It points into the heap, at Person 4. It does not point to the variable Voldemort.

118
00:12:11,135 --> 00:12:19,413
A static method has no this. So book 1 must name an object before the dot to call attack. Here it names Voldemort.

119
00:12:19,863 --> 00:12:27,799
Line 27: p dot mother equals null. Resolve p: it leads to Person 1. Take its field mother.

120
00:12:28,249 --> 00:12:34,627
Rule 3: erase the old mother arrow. The right hand side is null, so draw no new arrow.

121
00:12:35,077 --> 00:12:41,435
Line 28: p dot father equals null. Erase the father arrow of Person 1.

122
00:12:41,885 --> 00:12:47,751
The code of attack never names Harry. It changed the object of Harry through the alias p.

123
00:12:48,201 --> 00:12:53,428
attack returns. Rule 5: erase its frame, and the arrows this and p.

124
00:12:53,878 --> 00:12:57,185
The P C goes back to book 1, line 19.

125
00:12:57,635 --> 00:13:07,448
This is object diagram 2, at P C 19, as in the notes. The object of Harry has no father arrow and no mother arrow now.

126
00:13:07,898 --> 00:13:13,530
Person 2 and Person 3 still exist. James and Lily still point to them.

127
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:20,124
The second story is Superman's origin story. It has two classes, Planet and Person.

128
00:13:20,574 --> 00:13:27,294
A Person has three Person fields: father, mother and spouse. It has one Planet field: planet.

129
00:13:27,744 --> 00:13:31,669
The constructor copies its four parameters into the four fields.

130
00:13:32,119 --> 00:13:40,653
Draw object diagrams 1 to 4, at lines 12, 19, 32 and 35. Pause the video and try it.

131
00:13:41,103 --> 00:13:48,591
The main frame has a slot for every local variable, from Krypton to Martha Kent. All of them start as null.

132
00:13:49,041 --> 00:13:52,966
Line 4: new makes Planet 1, and Krypton points to it.

133
00:13:53,416 --> 00:13:57,235
Line 5: new makes Planet 2, and Earth points to it.

134
00:13:57,685 --> 00:14:00,693
Line 7: new Person makes Person 1.

135
00:14:01,143 --> 00:14:06,903
Then the constructor runs in its own frame. this points to the new object, Person 1.

136
00:14:07,353 --> 00:14:14,798
The arguments are null, null, null and Krypton. So f, m and s are null, and p points to Planet 1.

137
00:14:15,248 --> 00:14:25,104
Line 43 copies the parameters into the fields. Only planet gets an arrow, to Planet 1. The null parameters give no arrows.

138
00:14:25,554 --> 00:14:29,821
The constructor returns. Its frame and its arrows are erased.

139
00:14:30,271 --> 00:14:34,665
Then the assignment on line 7 draws Jor El to Person 1.

140
00:14:35,115 --> 00:14:40,427
Line 8 makes Lara, Person 2, with spouse Jor El and planet Krypton.

141
00:14:40,877 --> 00:14:44,333
Line 9 sets the spouse of Jor El to Lara.

142
00:14:44,783 --> 00:14:51,354
Line 11 makes Kal El, Person 3, with father Jor El, mother Lara and planet Krypton.

143
00:14:51,804 --> 00:14:56,860
This is object diagram 1, at P C 12. It matches the notes.

144
00:14:57,310 --> 00:15:05,673
Planet Krypton explodes. Line 15 sets Krypton, Jor El and Lara to null. Erase the three slot arrows.

145
00:15:06,123 --> 00:15:11,819
Line 16 sets the father of Kal El to null. Erase that arrow of Person 3.

146
00:15:12,269 --> 00:15:16,471
Line 17 erases the mother arrow of Person 3.

147
00:15:16,921 --> 00:15:20,889
Line 18 erases the planet arrow of Person 3.

148
00:15:21,339 --> 00:15:25,030
This is object diagram 2, at P C 19.

149
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:30,600
Person 1, Person 2 and Planet 1 are still in the heap, with their arrows.

150
00:15:31,050 --> 00:15:40,586
No slot can reach them now, so we draw them gray. Assigning null does not delete an object. The garbage collector decides when they go.

151
00:15:41,036 --> 00:15:47,095
Line 21: Kal El lands on Earth. Draw planet from Person 3 to Planet 2.

152
00:15:47,545 --> 00:15:53,582
Lines 23 and 24 make Jonathan Kent, Person 4, with planet Earth.

153
00:15:54,032 --> 00:16:01,584
Lines 25 and 26 make Martha Kent, Person 5, with planet Earth and spouse Jonathan Kent.

154
00:16:02,034 --> 00:16:06,855
Line 27 sets the spouse of Jonathan Kent to Martha Kent.

155
00:16:07,305 --> 00:16:15,775
Line 28: Clark Kent equals Kal El. No new, so no new bubble. Follow the arrow of Kal El to Person 3.

156
00:16:16,225 --> 00:16:22,198
Draw Clark Kent to the same object, Person 3. Now two names refer to one object.

157
00:16:22,648 --> 00:16:29,112
Line 29 goes through Clark Kent. It sets the father of Person 3 to Person 4.

158
00:16:29,562 --> 00:16:33,594
Line 30 sets the mother of Person 3 to Person 5.

159
00:16:34,044 --> 00:16:40,700
Line 31 sets Kal El to null. Erase that slot arrow. Person 3 does not change.

160
00:16:41,150 --> 00:16:45,011
This is object diagram 3, at P C 32.

161
00:16:45,461 --> 00:16:52,309
Line 34: Superman equals Clark Kent. Follow the arrow of Clark Kent to Person 3.

162
00:16:52,759 --> 00:16:58,413
Draw Superman to Person 3. The line has no new, so it makes no new object.

163
00:16:58,863 --> 00:17:02,745
This is object diagram 4, at P C 35.

164
00:17:03,195 --> 00:17:09,745
Two slots, one object. Clark Kent dot father and Superman dot father are the same field.

165
00:17:10,195 --> 00:17:15,656
Kal El was a third name for this object, from line 28 to line 31.

166
00:17:16,106 --> 00:17:22,613
Several names for one object is called aliasing. Aliasing is the topic of the next video.

167
00:17:23,063 --> 00:17:33,943
To summarize. An object diagram has the P C, stack frames with slots, with statics first, and objects in the heap. Every arrow ends in the heap.

168
00:17:34,393 --> 00:17:44,355
To execute an assignment: the right side gives an object. The left side gives a slot or a field. Then move one arrow. Null means no arrow.

169
00:17:44,805 --> 00:17:52,997
Only new makes a bubble. A call adds a frame, and a return erases the frame and its arrows. The objects stay.

170
00:17:53,447 --> 00:18:02,151
Java hides some things. Statics exist before main, and args is never null. Every instance method has a hidden this slot.

171
00:18:02,601 --> 00:18:12,201
An assignment copies an arrow. So two names can refer to one object. The next video is about this aliasing, and about mutation.
